# Trench Line

A top-down First World War trench-battle game. Pick one of eight nations, take the enemy's trenches one line at a time, and destroy their HQ before they overrun yours. Troops dug into a trench take a fraction of the damage open ground costs; kills earn you cards that unlock tanks, guns and tricks; and the longer the battle runs, the stronger the enemy grows.

Play it at https://cmdjc.github.io/trench-line/

## How to play

Buy troops with supplies, select a trench line, and send it forward with **Advance**. Men walk to the line, settle into the trench, and hold it. Bring stragglers with **Bring all here**, fall back when a line is lost, and double-tap a trench you hold to fortify it. Emplacements — MG nests and your nation's special defence — hold ground on their own. Once your kills fill the card meter you pick a card: unlocks, income, buffs, one-off boosts.

Every nation plays differently: Britain's wire and mines, Germany's Paris Gun and stormtroopers, France's Char 2C, Russia's Tsar Tank, Austria-Hungary's siege guns, Italy's Caproni bombers, America's Kettering bugs, and Antarctica's absurd Southern Colossus. Superweapons unlock after four minutes and change the battle; the enemy commander has his own.

Four battlefields: open ground, a river crossing with two bridges, a wide plain with double super trenches, and the Long March — 45 trenches with a super trench in the middle.

Research points carry between battles. Spend them on a permanent tree, or carry your progress with a save code.

## Controls

Drag the field or use the arrow keys to scroll. Tap a trench to select it, double-tap one you hold to fortify it, tap the button rows for everything else. The game is playable end to end with Tab and Enter.

## Running it locally

Install the dependencies from the lock file with `npm ci`.

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `npm run dev` | Starts the Vite dev server. |
| `npm run build` | Writes a static build to `dist/`. |
| `npm run preview` | Serves the built game locally. |
| `npm run test` | Runs the unit tests. |
| `npm run lint` | Runs ESLint over the whole project. |
| `npm run e2e` | Builds the game and plays it through a real page load in jsdom. |
| `npm run check` | Runs lint, the unit tests, and the build. |
| `npm run stats` | Regenerates the stat tables in `docs/STATS.md` from the game data. |
| `npm run bot` | Plays a scripted, seeded battle headlessly. `node scripts/bot.mjs de gb open 3`. |

## Tests

The unit tests cover the seeded random source, every data table (units, nations, tech, cards, maps), save codes and recovery, the state container, geometry and spawning, the damage model, fortifications, the simulation step (movement, rivers, hedgehogs, firing — including the fixed stray miss-tracer from the prototype), superweapons, the enemy commander, cards, commands, the tick loop, parity smoke tests of every nation on every map, the UI through a fake DOM, and the canvas renderer against a no-op context.

The e2e test builds the real page with esbuild, loads it in jsdom, and plays a full battle through real button clicks: picks, buying, advancing, double-tap fortifying, card picks, the Messines mine, a rigged victory, the save code, and a fresh run afterwards.

## Debug flag

Add `?debug=1` (or `?e2e=1`) to the game URL to expose `window.__trench`, a hook the e2e tests use to read and nudge the game. Without it, the game exposes no globals.

## Layout

All content lives in `src/data/`: nations (caps, traits, defence kinds), unit stats and tank variants, superweapons, the research tree, cards and maps. The simulation lives in `src/sim/` — state, geometry, combat, fortify, step, superweapons, the enemy commander, cards, commands and tick — and is deliberately browser-free: ESLint keeps DOM globals out of it, so the whole game runs headlessly for tests and the bot. The renderer (`src/ui/draw/`) and the HUD, overlays, input and loop (`src/ui/`) drive it through a small event bus. Battles are seeded: `newGame(mine, theirs, map, seed)` reproduces a battle exactly, which is what the parity suites and `scripts/bot.mjs` rely on.

## Deployment

A GitHub Actions workflow (`.github/workflows/pages.yml`) deploys the game to GitHub Pages on each push to `main`. It runs `npm run check` and `npm run e2e` first.

## Credits

Designed and authored by M K. Built with the help of the AI agents Claude (Anthropic) and GLM (Z.ai).

## License

Trench Line is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).
# Trench Line: handoff

A top-down First World War trench battle game: one HTML file, vanilla JS, a single `<canvas>`, no build step and no runtime dependencies. This document is the code map for whoever turns it into a proper JS/HTML project. What the game contains and why is in `DESIGN.md`; every number is in `STATS.md`.

## Files

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `trench-line.html` | **Source of truth.** The complete game (about 2,280 lines, 149 KB). |
| `artifact/trench-line.html` | The copy published as a claude.ai Artifact. Generated by `tools/make-artifact.py`; do not edit by hand. |
| `tests/harness.js` | Loads the game in jsdom and widens the `window.__trench` test hook. |
| `tests/smoke.js` | Every nation as player and enemy, all four maps; fails on page errors, NaN or armour-limit violations. |
| `tests/merge.js` | Wide open plain, hedgehogs, five-slot double trenches, Russia's line-up. |
| `tests/gen-stats.js` | Regenerates `docs/STATS.md` from the game data. |
| `tools/bot.js` | Scripted player that fights whole battles. Needs `jsdom` and `canvas`. |

Install once with `npm install jsdom @napi-rs/canvas canvas`.

## Code map

Everything is inside one IIFE in a single `<script>`. Sections are marked `/* ---------- name ---------- */`, in this order:

| Section | Key contents |
|---|---|
| constants | `UT` (base unit stats), `NU` (per-country overrides), `INF`, `TK`, `ORDER`, `DBL`/`isDbl`, `HEDGE` |
| tank variants | `TKB`, `tkv()`, one `Object.assign(NU.xx,{...})` per country, `STATS`, `tkList`, `tkPrev`, `SPECIAL`, `ut(side,k)`, `COV`, `ARM`, `NESTCOV`, `NAT`, `AI_*` |
| permanent research | `TECH`, `META`, `loadMeta`/`saveMeta`, `makeCode`/`readCode`, `applyTech` |
| state | `SW`, `MAPS`, `setLayout(key)`, `mods()`, `newGame(mine,theirs,map)` |
| geometry | `posX`, `trenchX`, `startX`, `spawn`, `count`, `tanks`, `full`, `countAt`, `power`, `frontLine` |
| combat | `hurt`, `accVs`, `die`, `stepUnits` (movement, bridges, hedgehogs, firing, shells, ownership, garrison, super trench) |
| fortifying and MG nests | `freeSlot`, `wireSlow`, `stepFields`, `buildDef`, `buildNest`, `stepJobs`, `fortify`, bunkers |
| superweapons | `targetFor`, `swBlock`, `useSW`, `detonate`, `stepSW`, Paris Gun aiming |
| enemy commander | `aiStep`, `aiThink` |
| cards | `CARDS`, `CARDTXT`, `relabelCards`, `offerCards` |
| orders, interface | `select`, `buildMap`, `buildBuy`, `refresh`, research and save-code UI, `renderStart`, `endGame` |
| simulation tick | `tick(dt)` |
| drawing | `draw`, `drawTankGen` (parts-list renderer), one `draw*` per hand-drawn unit and structure |
| camera input, loop | drag scrolling, `frame` |

## Conventions

- **Sides:** `0` is the player (left), `1` the enemy (right). `nation[side]` is the country code.
- **Lines:** `0` is the player's HQ, `1..NT` are trenches, `HQI` (= `NT+1`) is the enemy HQ. `LX[L]` is the x of line `L`. `setLayout` rebuilds `LX`, `NT`, `HQI`, `WW` per map; never hard-code 9 or 10.
- **Special trenches:** `SUPER` is one line index (the Long March's neutral super trench, or `-1`). `DBL` is a list of double trenches (Wide open plain). They have different rules; see `DESIGN.md`.
- **Unit:** `{side,type,T,line,x,y,ly,hp,max,cd,settled,moving,fixed,park,...}`. `T` is the resolved stat object; read stats from `u.T`. `fixed` marks units that ignore orders (emplacements, sappers). `ly` is the home lane; `y` wanders only to cross bridges.
- **Armour:** `T.armor` marks vehicles. `T.w` is how much of the nation's armour limit one uses. `T.sup` marks superweapon vehicles, which do not count. `T.big` marks giants that ford rivers and roll over hedgehogs. `T.draw` is a parts list for `drawTankGen`; without it `drawTank` falls back to a hand-drawn function chosen by country.
- **Attack type:** damage tables are keyed by `T.atk || u.type`. Anything that is not one of the six base types must set `atk`, or lookups return `undefined` and damage becomes `NaN`.
- **Modifiers:** `M[side]` from `mods()` holds multipliers changed by traits, cards and research. Any new unit type key must be added to the list in `mods()`.
- **World size:** 300 units high; `K` scales it to the canvas. `cam` is the left edge in world units.
- **Test hook:** `window.__trench` exposes `units() fort() fields() jobs() sw() step(sec) draw() info()`; `tests/harness.js` injects more. Keep it, or an equivalent, behind a debug flag.
- **Storage:** the source uses `window.storage` (an async key-value API in the chat preview). The Artifact copy falls back to `localStorage`. Pick one adapter.
- **Saves:** research only. Two slots (`trenchline-meta`, `trenchline-meta-b`), newest valid wins. Save code `TL1-<points>-<techMask>-<runs>-<wins>-<check>` in base 36; `techMask` follows `TECH` declaration order, so append nodes only or bump the prefix.

## Suggested path to a proper project

1. Lift `<style>` and `<script>` into `styles.css` and ES modules, one per section above.
2. Put the mutable state (`units`, `S`, `M`, `owner`, `fort`, `fields`, `jobs`) in one module so the simulation runs without a DOM.
3. Separate simulation from UI: `toast()` and `refresh()` are called from simulation code; route them through events.
4. Move data (`UT`, `NU`, `NAT`, `SW`, `CARDS`, `TECH`, `MAPS`) into plain data files.
5. Replace `Math.random()` in the simulation with a seeded generator. Runs are not repeatable today, which makes balance testing noisy.
6. Call the headless simulation directly from tests in place of jsdom.

## Not verified

- Nothing has been played by hand. Touch scrolling, double-tap to fortify, Paris Gun aiming and the scrolling trench strip are untested on a phone.
- Balance: scripted results swing widely between runs. See "Known quirks" in `DESIGN.md`.
- The save-code and two-slot recovery tests from an earlier session were lost and have not been rewritten. The merge did not touch that code.

## How the owner likes to work

They ask to be asked two or three short multiple-choice questions before each new feature, and answer quickly. They care about historical flavour: named units, real superweapons, distinct art per country.
